[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m It has no error path: it never writes to stderr, and `main` always returns 0. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m only ways its observable behaviour varies are the two entries below.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — exit status when stdout's reader is gone (FIXED)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout connected to a pipe whose read end is already closed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | program | exit status | stdout | stderr |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`) | empty | empty |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before fix) | exited 0 | empty | empty |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduced 20/20 runs in both directions, so this was deterministic, not a race.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs. A
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failed write therefore returns `EPIPE`, the return value is discarded exactly as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C discards `printf`'s, and the process exits 0. The C program inherits the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m default disposition and is killed by the signal instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is precisely the failure the task description warns about: stdout was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical (empty in both cases), so a stdout-only comparison passed while
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the exit status disagreed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now calls `restore_default_sigpipe()` as the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first statement of `main`, declaring `signal` from libc directly (no new
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m dependency) and installing `SIG_DFL` for `SIGPIPE`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Related check, no change needed.** With file descriptor 1 *closed* rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m piped (`exec ./driver >&-`), `printf` fails with `EBADF`, no signal is raised and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both programs exit 0 — already matching.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Also changed while fixing this.** The Rust program now formats both lines into
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m one buffer and issues a single `write_all`, matching C stdio's full buffering on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a non-terminal stdout (glibc flushes both lines together at exit). Previously the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m line-buffered `Stdout` wrapper emitted `A: …` and `x: …` as separate writes, so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reader that disappeared between the two lines would have received different bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from the two programs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Regression check.** Commenting out the `restore_default_sigpipe()` call makes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `differential::stdout_reader_gone` fail with "exit status differs with a dead
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout reader"; restoring it makes it pass. Verified, then reverted.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 2 — the C program reads out of bounds on a completely full buffer (not fixable in Rust; test strategy documented)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** For any input of >= 1000 bytes whose first 1000 bytes contain no NUL,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C program's output is not a function of its input. Tallying 600 runs of the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m binary on the same 1000-byte input (`'A' * 1000`):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A: 1000  x: 0   -> 590 runs   (the in-bounds answer)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A: 1000  x: 1   ->   7 runs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A: 1001  x: 0   ->   3 runs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust program prints `A: 1000 / x: 0` on 100% of runs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is why the suite was briefly flaky: `length_boundaries_all_a` failed once
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with C reporting `x: 1`, then passed on re-run.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** From the disassembly of the built binary:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m main:  sub $0x3f0,%rsp          ; frame is 1008 bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m        ...                      ; zero-fills exactly 0x3e8 = 1000 bytes at rbp-0x3f0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m        mov $0x3e8,%edx ; call fread
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `in` lives at `rbp-0x3f0` and is 1000 bytes long, so it ends exactly at `rbp-8`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `char in[1000] = ""` zero-fills precisely those 1000 bytes. When `fread` fills all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m of them and none is NUL, nothing inside the buffer terminates the string, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strchr` keeps scanning into:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. the 8 bytes of frame padding at `rbp-8 .. rbp-1`, which are never written by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    this program (leftovers from the dynamic loader and libc startup), and then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. the saved frame pointer at `[rbp]` — a stack address, randomised by ASLR on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    every execution.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The scan stops at the first zero byte it finds there. Whether an `0x41` ('A') or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0x78` ('x') appears first depends on those bytes, i.e. on ASLR and on residue the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input does not control. The observed ~1.2% rate for one extra match matches what
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m you would expect from roughly three random bytes (3/256).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Why the Rust program cannot reproduce it.** The extra matches come from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m uninitialised memory and a randomised address inside the C process. No
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deterministic Rust program can predict them, and the C binary does not agree with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *itself* across runs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **What the Rust program does instead.** It produces the in-bounds answer, which is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C program's output on ~98% of runs and its only stable behaviour.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **How this is tested.** Inputs are split by whether the C program stays inside its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffer (`common::reads_out_of_bounds`):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - fewer than 1000 bytes read, or a NUL inside the first 1000 bytes → strict
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   byte-for-byte comparison of stdout, stderr and exit status
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`assert_same`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - 1000+ bytes with no NUL → `assert_same_modal`, which runs the C program 15 times,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   takes its modal stdout, and requires the Rust program to equal it and to be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   deterministic; stderr and exit status are still compared strictly on every run
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `tests/oob_characterization.rs` pins the finding down directly: the Rust output
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m never varies over 60 runs, the C program's modal output is the in-bounds answer,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and its deviations only ever *over*-count (out-of-bounds bytes can add matches,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m never remove them). It also asserts that a 999-byte input is fully deterministic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in the C, confirming the buffer boundary is the trigger.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Checked and already correct (no change required)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified over the enumerated cases plus a 300-case seeded fuzz and a separate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3000-run ad-hoc sweep:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`fread` is not line oriented.** `A\nx\nAAxx\n` yields `A: 3 / x: 3`; the read
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   continues across newlines, unlike `fgets`/`scanf`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Zero fill supplies the terminator.** For 0..999 bytes read, `in[filled]` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   still 0, so the C string is exactly the bytes read.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Embedded NUL truncates.** `A\0x` gives `A: 1 / x: 0`; everything from the first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   NUL on is invisible to `strchr`, including a NUL at byte 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Truncation at 1000 bytes.** Input beyond `sizeof(in)` is discarded: matches at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   byte 1001 and later are never counted, and the writer's `EPIPE` when the child
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   exits early is harmless.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`s++` after each hit.** Adjacent matches (`AA`, `AxAxAx`) and a match on the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   final byte are counted correctly; after the last match `s` lands on the NUL and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the next `strchr` returns NULL.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Case and byte sensitivity.** `a` and `X` are not counted; bytes >= 0x80 are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   irrelevant because the searched-for `'A'`/`'x'` are positive in a signed `char`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Output format.** `"A: %d\n"` then `"x: %d\n"`, in that order, with a trailing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   newline and no stderr output.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argv` is ignored.** `main` takes no parameters; extra arguments change nothing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Unreadable stdin.** With a directory as stdin, `fread` fails, `in` keeps its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   zero fill, and both programs print `A: 0 / x: 0` and exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Slowly arriving stdin.** Feeding input in 1-, 137-, 250- and 300-byte chunks
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   keeps both programs' read loops going until the buffer is full or EOF.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Integer width.** `res` is a C `int`, but it is bounded by 1000, so no overflow
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is reachable; the Rust side still uses a wrapping `i32` add.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
