[1mMismatch 1 — exit status on a dead stdout reader (fixed).[22m With stdout piped to a reader that has already exited, the C program is killed by [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m (status −13) while the Rust program exited 0, because Rust's runtime installs [38;5;10mSIG_IGN[0m for [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m before [38;5;10mmain[0m. stdout was empty in both cases, so this is exactly the failure a stdout-only check misses. [38;5;10mtranslation/src/main.rs[0m now restores [38;5;10mSIG_DFL[0m for [38;5;10mSIGPIPE[0m (declaring [38;5;10msignal[0m from libc, no new dependency). I confirmed the new test catches the regression by disabling the call, watching [38;5;10mstdout_reader_gone[0m fail, and reverting. While there, I also made the Rust program emit both lines in a single [38;5;10mwrite_all[0m to match glibc's full buffering on a non-terminal stdout, so a reader vanishing between the two lines sees the same bytes from both programs.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mMismatch 2 — the C reads out of bounds (cannot be replicated).[22m [38;5;10min[0m sits at [38;5;10mrbp-0x3f0[0m with size [38;5;10m0x3e8[0m, ending exactly at [38;5;10mrbp-8[0m, and [38;5;10mchar in[1000] = ""[0m zeroes precisely those 1000 bytes. When [38;5;10mfread[0m fills all of them with no NUL, [38;5;10mstrchr[0m walks into 8 bytes of never-written frame padding and then the saved [38;5;10mrbp[0m — a stack address randomised by ASLR. Over 600 runs on identical input the C printed the in-bounds [38;5;10mA: 1000 / x: 0[0m 590 times, [38;5;10mx: 1[0m 7 times, and [38;5;10mA: 1001[0m 3 times. So for inputs of ≥1000 bytes with no NUL, the C is not a function of its input; this is what made the suite flaky once before I understood it. The Rust program produces the stable in-bounds answer, and those inputs are compared against the C's modal output (stderr and exit status still compared strictly on every run), with [38;5;10mtests/oob_characterization.rs[0m pinning down that the C's deviations only ever over-count and that a 999-byte input is fully deterministic.[0m[0m
